   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the social services law, in relation to the duty to report\nincidents",
    "basePrintNo": "S4521",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S4521-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §491, Soc Serv L",
    "lawSection": "Social Services Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S4521\n\nSPONSOR: BROUK\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the state finance law, the public authorities law and\nthe executive law, in relation to requiring the purchae of personal\nprotective equipment and medical supplies produced or manufactured in\nthe state of New York in certain circumstances.\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThis legislation is meant to provide clarity and uniformity to all\nappropriate state agencies on reporting requirements that have already\nbeen adopted by the Office of Mental Health and by the Office of Alco-\nholism and Substance Abuse Services as it relates to the Justice Center.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one amends section 491 of the social services law to allow a\nmandated reporter to refrain from filing an incident report when\n\n(i) the reporter has actual knowledge that the reportable incident has\nalready been reported to the register; and\n\n(ii) that the reporter has been named as a person with knowledge of the\nincident in such prior report. Section two establishes the effective\ndate.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nEmployees from the six state oversight agencies under the jurisdiction\nof the Justice Center for the Protection of People with Special Needs\n(Justice Center) are mandated to report an incident if directly\nobserved, or when said employee receives notice from another person that\nprovides the mandated reporter with reasonable cause to suspect that an\nindividual was subjected to a reportable incident. These reporting\nrequirements have led to a higher rate of vigilance and observance, but\nhave also led to duplicity.\n\nCurrently, when an incident occurs in front of a group of mandated\nreporters, each of those employees are statutorily required to file a\nreport. This multiple reporting requirement has been found to be burden-\nsome and has led to a slowdown of processing and/or prosecution.\n\nThis legislation would allow a mandated reporter to refrain from filing\na report only in cases where it has been confirmed to them that an inci-\ndent report was already filed, and in cases where said reporter is list-\ned as a witness in the incident report.\n\nThese changes to reporting requirements have already been adopted by the\nOffice of Mental Health and by the Office of Alcoholism and Substance\nAbuse Services with the approval of the Justice Center.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2021-2022: Passed Senate. Committed to Rules.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:-\nNone to the state.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
